# Modulation of laser diodes {#sec-modulation}

[![Open in Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/{{< var github_user >}}/{{< var repo >}}/blob/main/{{< var subdir >}}/notebooks/modulation.ipynb){.colab-link} &nbsp;Run this chapter's code in Google Colab (no installation).


*Slides 162–163 (direct modulation, small-signal response and relaxation-oscillation frequency) and 164 (digital modulation:
turn-on delay, relaxation oscillations, biasing near threshold).*

## Key relations

The output of a laser diode follows its current, so the simplest transmitter modulates the current directly (slide 162): a DC
bias $I_1$ above threshold plus a modulation $\Delta I_m\sin(2\pi ft)$. The rate equations of @sec-ld-equation couple two
reservoirs, carriers ($\tau$) and photons ($\tau_{ph}$). Like a mass on a spring they exchange energy and oscillate at the
**relaxation-oscillation frequency** (slide 163)

$$
f_r\approx\frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{\tau\tau_{ph}}}\left(\frac{I_1}{I_{th}}-1\right)^{1/2}.
$$

Linearising the rate equations gives the small-signal response, a damped second-order system,

$$
H(f)=\frac{\Delta P(f)}{\Delta P(0)}=\frac{\omega_r^2}{\omega_r^2-\omega^2+j\omega\gamma},\qquad \omega_r=2\pi f_r,\qquad
\gamma=\frac{I_1/I_{th}}{\tau}.
$$

It is flat at low frequency, peaks near $f_r$ and falls at 40 dB/decade above. Raising the bias raises $f_r$ and the bandwidth
(slide 163). For large-signal (digital) modulation from $I_1<I_{th}$ to $I_2>I_{th}$, the carriers must first build up to $n_{th}$
before any stimulated emission appears (slide 164). With $dn/dt=I_2/eV-n/\tau$ this takes the **turn-on delay**

$$
t_d=\tau\ln\frac{I_2-I_1}{I_2-I_{th}} .
$$

Biasing just below or at threshold ($I_1\to I_{th}$) removes the delay.

::: {.callout-warning collapse="true"}
## The turn-on delay formula in the slide-164 notes
The speaker notes give $t_d=\tau\ln[I_2/(I_2-(I_{th}-I_1))]$. Integrating $dn/dt=I_2/eV-n/\tau$ from $n_1=I_1\tau/eV$ to
$n_{th}=I_{th}\tau/eV$ gives $t_d=\tau\ln[(I_2-I_1)/(I_2-I_{th})]$. The two agree only for $I_1=0$. For $I_1=0.8I_{th}$,
$I_2=2I_{th}$ the notes give $0.11\tau$ and the correct value is $0.18\tau$.
:::

## Small-signal response (slide 163)

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
import {ldSimulate} from "./laser_sim.js"
viewof ss = Inputs.form({
  Ith: Inputs.range([1, 50], {value: 2, step: 0.5, label: "threshold current I_th (mA)"}),
  tau: Inputs.range([0.2, 5], {value: 1, step: 0.05, label: "carrier lifetime τ (ns)"}),
  tph: Inputs.range([0.5, 10], {value: 1.5, step: 0.1, label: "photon lifetime τ_ph (ps)"}),
  I1: Inputs.range([1, 100], {value: 15, step: 0.5, label: "bias current I₁ (mA)"})
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
ssOut = {
  const tau = ss.tau*1e-9, tph = ss.tph*1e-12, H = (I1, f) => { const w = 2*Math.PI*f, wr2 = (I1/ss.Ith - 1)/(tau*tph), g = (I1/ss.Ith)/tau;
    return 10*Math.log10(wr2*wr2/((wr2 - w*w)**2 + (w*g)**2)); };
  const data = [], biases = [ss.I1/3, ss.I1, 2*ss.I1].filter(I => I > ss.Ith*1.01);
  for (const I of biases) for (let i = 0; i <= 300; i++) { const f = 10 ** (8 + 3*i/300); data.push({f: f/1e9, H: H(I, f), I: `I₁ = ${I.toFixed(1)} mA`}); }
  const fr = Math.sqrt(Math.max(ss.I1/ss.Ith - 1, 0)/(tau*tph))/(2*Math.PI);
  let f3 = NaN; for (let i = 0; i <= 3000; i++) { const f = 10 ** (8 + 3*i/3000); if (H(ss.I1, f) < -3) { f3 = f; break; } }
  return {data, fr, f3, peak: d3.max(data.filter(d => d.I === `I₁ = ${ss.I1.toFixed(1)} mA`), d => d.H)};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.min(width, 1050), height: 300, x: {label: "modulation frequency (GHz)", type: "log"}, y: {label: "|ΔP(f)/ΔP(0)| (dB)", domain: [-20, 12], grid: true},
  color: {legend: true}, marks: [Plot.line(ssOut.data, {x: "f", y: "H", stroke: "I", strokeWidth: 2}), Plot.ruleY([-3], {stroke: "#52514e", strokeDasharray: "3,3"})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
html`<div class="result-box">at I₁ = ${ss.I1} mA: f<sub>r</sub> = <b>${(ssOut.fr/1e9).toFixed(2)} GHz</b> · resonance peak ${ssOut.peak.toFixed(1)} dB ·
−3 dB bandwidth <b>${(ssOut.f3/1e9).toFixed(2)} GHz</b> (≈ 1.55 f<sub>r</sub> for a weakly damped laser) ·
NRZ bit rate roughly up to ${(1.4*ssOut.f3/1e9).toFixed(0)} Gb/s</div>`
```

